  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B052",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B002",
    "B040"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks remain the strongest combination of a distinct physical principle, decisive experimental realization, and mature independent replication. Magic-wavelength confinement makes it possible to interrogate many atoms with extraordinary precision without the trapping field compromising the clock transition. This is already a fundamental advance in controlled atomic quantum systems and precision tests, independent of future standards decisions or prospective applications. Katori and Ye remain a concise and defensible attribution to the central conceptual and experimental contributions.",
    "case_against": "The broader development of optical frequency metrology and the highest-performance lattice-clock systems involved further important contributors, so the historical boundary must remain the lattice-clock architecture rather than the entire field. Its most visible consequences beyond metrology are still developing; the citation should not claim credit for those future outcomes.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce gives priority to IceCube for opening high-energy neutrino astronomy, while recognizing that Francis Halzen alone may compress a collaboration-scale discovery too severely.",
        "response": "I agree that a new astronomical messenger is a profound achievement and that the accumulated IceCube observations establish real maturity. But the attribution reservation is central rather than incidental: detector construction, calibration, analysis, and discovery are inseparable in this case. B052 offers a comparably mature experimental advance whose two credited pioneers can be connected much more directly to its defining physical idea and realization."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson favors DMFT because it is a mature, quantitatively productive framework for correlated materials, while noting that its development extends beyond the proposed three names.",
        "response": "I accept the importance and long experimental relevance of DMFT. Its broad theoretical lineage and the need to delimit its limits for nonlocal correlations, however, make the exact allocation less settled. Optical lattice clocks have the unusually direct chain from a solution to a specific systematic problem to repeatedly reproduced precision measurements, which I weight more highly here."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson argues that WMAP transformed empirical cosmology but identifies difficult boundaries from earlier CMB recognition and a necessarily compressed collaboration attribution.",
        "response": "WMAP is a formidable alternative and its finite body of observations had transformational scientific force. I share the need for a sharply defensible boundary from prior CMB recognition, as well as concern about representing a major collaboration. The demonstrated atomic physics of B052 is more self-contained and its credit allocation is correspondingly clearer."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review fully supports limiting credit for optical lattice clocks to Katori and Ye rather than recognizing additional indispensable early contributors.",
      "Whether the citation should explicitly mention the magic-wavelength principle or retain the broader formulation of optical lattice clocks."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict is apparent from the supplied profile. I have long-standing collaborations in attosecond physics, but none is identified with the proposed laureates or achievement."
  }
}
